      <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Cities like Sao Paulo (58.4%, $700/mo salary), Buenos Aires (57.1%, $550/mo), and Manila (55.1%, $500/mo) combine low wages with rents that consume over half of monthly income. In these cities, the absolute dollar rent is low (~$455–770), but it is unaffordable relative to income — a structural trap that differs fundamentally from the affordability pressure in wealthy cities.</p>
