<!-- ==================== SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS ==================== -->
<div class="section" id="findings">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-number">Section 6</div>
    <h2>Key Findings and Policy Implications</h2>
    <div class="section-desc">Synthesis of the most actionable insights for policy analysts and economic researchers. All findings are derived directly from the 2026 dataset.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:20px;">
    <h3>Six Priority Findings</h3>
    <div style="margin-top:16px;">
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">1</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>Housing burden is universal and structurally unresolved</h4>
          <p>Zero cities in the 80-city sample have a rent-to-salary ratio below 50%. The global average is 61.5%, and 27 cities (34%) exceed 65%. This is not a problem confined to wealthy cities or poor ones — it spans all tiers and regions, suggesting a structural mismatch between housing supply and wage growth globally. <strong>Policy priority:</strong> housing supply expansion in both affluent cities (Western Europe, North America) and rapidly urbanizing emerging market cities (South Asia, Southeast Asia).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">2</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>Switzerland dominates global cost rankings with a structural premium</h4>
          <p>All five Swiss cities (Zurich, Geneva, Basel, Lausanne, Bern) rank 1–5 globally by cost of living index (110–118.5), each exceeding New York by 10–18.5 points. Yet these cities carry the lowest inflation rates in the sample (1.2%) and highest purchasing power (157–184). The Swiss cost premium is fully wage-compensated — it does not constitute a crisis but warrants monitoring for inequality between resident income groups.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">3</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>14 cities face acute crises requiring immediate intervention</h4>
          <p>Tier 5 cities — including Buenos Aires (150% inflation), Beirut (50% inflation, $35 PPP index), Lagos (34% inflation, $380/mo salary), Cairo ($165/mo salary), and nine South Asian cities — represent genuine affordability emergencies. Average purchasing power in this tier is 48.0 (less than half of New York's), average salary is $455/month, and average inflation is 28.4%. These cities share a common profile: high oil shock exposure (most are "High"), currency instability, and wages insufficient to cover basic housing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">4</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>Latin America is the most inflationary region — by a wide margin</h4>
          <p>Latin America's average annual inflation rate of 34.2% dwarfs all other regions, driven by Buenos Aires' 150% hyperinflation outlier but reinforced by Bogota (7.5%), Sao Paulo (5.0%), and Mexico City (4.8%). This is compounded by the region's lowest average purchasing power (50.2) — meaning residents face rapid price increases on already-stretched incomes. <strong>Policy focus:</strong> monetary stabilization, currency reform, and social safety nets calibrated to inflation velocity.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">5</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>High oil shock exposure cities face a compounded vulnerability trap</h4>
          <p>23 cities classified as "High" oil shock exposure average 20.4% annual inflation — 6× higher than Low-exposure cities (3.4%). These cities simultaneously have the lowest average cost of living index (45.1) and lowest purchasing power (67.8). Energy price shocks are thus most damaging precisely where household budgets are already thinnest. <strong>Policy focus:</strong> energy subsidy reform with compensatory income transfers, diversification of energy supply.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">6</div>
        <div class="finding-body">
          <h4>Singapore and Tel Aviv are structural outliers that merit independent policy treatment</h4>
          <p>Singapore (CoL 87.7 vs. Southeast Asia avg 51.3; deviation: +36.4 pts) and Tel Aviv (CoL 91.4 vs. Middle East avg 59.1; deviation: +32.3 pts) are the two largest regional outliers by deviation. Singapore's position reflects its city-state economic model; Tel Aviv's reflects a War Zone Premium layered on an already high-cost economy. Both cities require region-specific analysis rather than regional aggregation for policy purposes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
